speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I take this opportunity to meet a commitment which I made to a group of my constituents who were visiting the Byward Market in my riding, to present a petition which complies with Standing Order 106 and which reads as follows:
Whereas, since September, 1984, the federal Government has imposed on Canadian taxpayers 24 new taxes or tax increases, in particular on medical and dental supplies, snack foods, candy and soft drinks;
Whereas the federal Government in its tax reform proposal has studied the possibility of imposing on Canadians a general tax on goods and services, including food;
And whereas food constitutes a vital element of day-to-day life and imposition of this tax would have the effect of seriously penalizing certain categories of taxpayers, such as the aged, families, the unemployed, low and middle income taxpayers, and youth;
Wherefore your petitioners humbly pray and call upon Parliament to reject all measures that would have the effect of imposing a sales tax on food products.

WHEREAS the Constitution Act, 1982 came into force on April 17, 1982, following an agreement between Canada and all the provinces except Quebec;
AND WHEREAS the Government of Quebec has established a set of five proposals for constitutional change and has stated that amendments to give effect to those proposals would enable Quebec to resume a full role in the constitutional councils of Canada;
AND WHEREAS the amendment proposed in the schedule hereto sets out the basis on which Quebec's five constitutional proposals may be met;
AND WHEREAS the amendment proposed in the schedule hereto also recognizes the principle of the equality of all the provinces, provides new arrangements to foster greater harmony and cooperation between the Government of Canada and the governments of the provinces and requires that conferences be convened to consider important constitutional, economic and other issues;
AND WHEREAS certain portions of the amendment proposed in the schedule hereto relate to matters referred to in section 41 of the Constitution Act, 1982;
AND WHEREAS section 41 of the Constitution Act, 1982 provides that an amendment to the Constitution of Canada may be made by proclamation issued by the Governor General under the Great Seal of Canada where so authorized by resolutions of the Senate and the House of Commons and of the legislative assembly of each province;
NOW THEREFORE the House of Commons resolves that an amendment to the Constitution of Canada be authorized to be made by proclamation issued by Her Excellency the Governor General under the Great Seal of Canada in accordance with the schedule hereto.

Mr. Speaker, 1 rise to congratulate the efforts of Southam News in bringing to the attention of Canadians our serious and continuing national problem of illiteracy. By assigning a first rate investigative journalist, Peter Calamai, and backing him with research and survey information, Southam was able to present the following well-documented facts about illiteracy:
Fully five million adult Canadians are functionally illiterate;
The problem is increasing with 100,000 illiterates being added to the population every year;
Less than 2 per cent of illiterates are enrolled in adequate literacy programs.
None of us can feel anything but shame that our educational and training facilities are failing so many. Yet the federal Government, which has only a few token programs, is not responding to the disaster. Where are the federal-provincial agreements to attack this growing problem?
I call on the cabinet Ministers responsible, particularly the Secretary of State (Mr. Crombie) and the Minister of Employment and Immigration (Mr. Bouchard), to give a detailed response to the information uncovered by Southam and tell the House how they will respond positively to this greatest of adult education challenges, providing the necessary learning skills leading to the simple ability to cope in our exceedingly complex society.

